[[:db/cas 17592186345244 :question/problem "$6\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle 6\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186345246 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ along the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ along the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186345246 :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta | \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta |=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ because $\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta >0.$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-0-\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$ \n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta | \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta |=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ because $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta >0.\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-0-\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186345250 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186345251 :question/problem "Express $8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)$ as a single trigonometric ratio." "Express $$\n\\displaystyle 8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)\n$$ as a single trigonometric ratio."] [:db/cas 17592186345261 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2},\\,2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=2.$" "Find the arc length of the curve $$\n\\displaystyle r(t)=⟨2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2},\\,2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩\n$$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186345263 :question/problem "Express $\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}{sin}^{2}⁡{36}^{∘}$ as a single trigonometric ratio." "Express $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}{sin}^{2}⁡{36}^{∘}\n$$ as a single trigonometric ratio."] [:db/cas 17592186345267 :question/problem "![](images/de/dea7eed9213ddf1bb3293950e2490b120a061e9add1172d3b850ccbc58f90454.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral ${\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(y+x)\\,i+x\\,j$ and $C$ is the arc of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=4$ traversed from the point $A(-2,0)$ to the point $B(0,-1).$" "![](images/de/dea7eed9213ddf1bb3293950e2490b120a061e9add1172d3b850ccbc58f90454.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=(y+x)\\,i+x\\,j$ and $C$ is the arc of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=4$ traversed from the point $A(-2,0)$ to the point $B(0,-1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186345268 :question/problem "![](images/08/08c3195e7325d853f61562b1f62460cb0796f14377286228f2b7cd40983202ac.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral ${\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=3\\,i+\\frac{1}{x}\\,j$ and $C$ is the arc of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$  traversed from the point $A(4,0)$ to the point $B(0,4).$" "![](images/08/08c3195e7325d853f61562b1f62460cb0796f14377286228f2b7cd40983202ac.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=3\\,i+\\frac{1}{x}\\,j$ and $C$ is the arc of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$  traversed from the point $A(4,0)$ to the point $B(0,4).$"] [:db/cas 17592186345269 :question/problem "![](images/00/00547d8271d0c277de2bd1992d759e0157a80c9caf05cfb1020550853ae7b8b3.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral ${\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(x+y)\\,i+(1-x)\\,j$ and $C$ is the arc of the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36$  traversed from the point $A(0,3)$ to the point $B(-2,0).$" "![](images/00/00547d8271d0c277de2bd1992d759e0157a80c9caf05cfb1020550853ae7b8b3.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=(x+y)\\,i+(1-x)\\,j$ and $C$ is the arc of the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36$  traversed from the point $A(0,3)$ to the point $B(-2,0).$"] [:db/cas 17592186345273 :question/problem "![](images/a4/a42c95d0604d9855fb00ac9b7f2a1d2f9db0dd9c6beb3b68750a074818c99dc8.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $ODEO,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the counterclockwise direction?" "![](images/a4/a42c95d0604d9855fb00ac9b7f2a1d2f9db0dd9c6beb3b68750a074818c99dc8.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $ODEO,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the counterclockwise direction?"] [:db/cas 17592186345274 :question/problem "![](images/54/5484647a9c694b95bd9e4b569c84f7263c5e69de5e98f12a1081fd937d298769.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $KMLK,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the **clockwise** direction?" "![](images/54/5484647a9c694b95bd9e4b569c84f7263c5e69de5e98f12a1081fd937d298769.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $KMLK,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the **clockwise** direction?"] [:db/cas 17592186345275 :question/problem "![](images/31/3121d24ea2b9eac3d95c34202619800331d4719c786b8f5a1051350370a1bea2.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $TUVT,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the **clockwise** direction?" "![](images/31/3121d24ea2b9eac3d95c34202619800331d4719c786b8f5a1051350370a1bea2.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $TUVT,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the **clockwise** direction?"] [:db/cas 17592186345276 :question/problem "![](images/80/801321d1639e668c3778c198df9a5b98ea3e6018f12ab2e5c756bfa4f04cb574.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $OGHO,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the counterclockwise direction?" "![](images/80/801321d1639e668c3778c198df9a5b98ea3e6018f12ab2e5c756bfa4f04cb574.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $OGHO,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the counterclockwise direction?"] [:db/cas 17592186345277 :question/problem "![](images/fe/fec5c3e96eeed9fb01479c0127e2d530a628855a124d8ab1ef380c48c2f569f1.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $LMNL,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the counterclockwise direction?" "![](images/fe/fec5c3e96eeed9fb01479c0127e2d530a628855a124d8ab1ef380c48c2f569f1.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $LMNL,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the counterclockwise direction?"] [:db/cas 17592186345283 :question/problem "![](images/93/93c6184f39b28a057866bb02e11e5e81a7abea4ff8a9a907670dd30cfc93d557.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(1,-1)$ to the point $B(1,1),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $B(1,1)$ to the point $O(0,0).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2,$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(2x+y)\\,i+(x-2y)\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" "![](images/93/93c6184f39b28a057866bb02e11e5e81a7abea4ff8a9a907670dd30cfc93d557.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(1,-1)$ to the point $B(1,1),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $B(1,1)$ to the point $O(0,0).$\n\nGiven that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2,\n$$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=(2x+y)\\,i+(x-2y)\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186345284 :question/problem "![](images/60/6087da076e65bda5240c428ff67c570ad59ae9a8e5a5a3c9e8406184998de56a.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a the line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(1,0),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(1,0)$ to the point $B(0,-2).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{7}{3},$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=({x}^{2}+y)\\,i+(x-{y}^{2})\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" "![](images/60/6087da076e65bda5240c428ff67c570ad59ae9a8e5a5a3c9e8406184998de56a.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a the line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(1,0),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(1,0)$ to the point $B(0,-2).$\n\nGiven that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{7}{3},\n$$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=({x}^{2}+y)\\,i+(x-{y}^{2})\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186345285 :question/problem "![](images/c0/c0a4f042748ddadd824bed4ee71bca9724d7ca7dcc376a0ccf7c1353d59ec4fc.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(-2,2)$ to the point $B(-1,2),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $B(-1,2)$ to the point $D(-1,0).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-\\frac{7}{2},$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(x-y)\\,i+(y-x)\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" "![](images/c0/c0a4f042748ddadd824bed4ee71bca9724d7ca7dcc376a0ccf7c1353d59ec4fc.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(-2,2)$ to the point $B(-1,2),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $B(-1,2)$ to the point $D(-1,0).$\n\nGiven that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-\\frac{7}{2},\n$$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=(x-y)\\,i+(y-x)\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186345286 :question/problem "![](images/04/0457cb819e6f39ef9c0978e356cff788e1cf5edbef9cbf844a054ab777b70eac.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a the line segment from the point $A(-2,0)$ to the point $B(0,1),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of the line segment from the point $B(0,1)$ to the point $D(1,1).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-\\frac{3}{2},$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(x-2y)\\,i+(y-x)\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" "![](images/04/0457cb819e6f39ef9c0978e356cff788e1cf5edbef9cbf844a054ab777b70eac.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a the line segment from the point $A(-2,0)$ to the point $B(0,1),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of the line segment from the point $B(0,1)$ to the point $D(1,1).$\n\nGiven that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-\\frac{3}{2},\n$$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=(x-2y)\\,i+(y-x)\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186345291 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186345293 :question/worked-solution "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}|}{3} \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}| & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is $|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}|=-6$ only." "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}|}{3} \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}| & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}|=-6\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186345294 :question/worked-solution "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =12+1 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & k \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}3 & k \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|}{13} \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3 & k \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is $|\\begin{aligned}3 & k \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|=13$ only." "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =12+1 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & k \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}3 & k \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|}{13} \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3 & k \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}3 & k \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|=13\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186345298 :question/problem "If $P=QR$, where \n$Q=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 6 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$R=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${p}_{34}=$" "If $P=QR$, where \n$$\n\\displaystyle Q=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 6 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand \n$R=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${p}_{34}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186345299 :question/problem "What is the minor of ${k}_{22}$ in the matrix\n$K=\\begin{bmatrix}p & p & -p \\\\ -q & q & q \\\\ q & -2 & q\\end{bmatrix}?$" "What is the minor of ${k}_{22}$ in the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle K=\\begin{bmatrix}p & p & -p \\\\ -q & q & q \\\\ q & -2 & q\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186345305 :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -4 & 3\\end{aligned}|=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -4 & 3\\end{aligned}|=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186345311 :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 9 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 0.5 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0.2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 12 & 4\\end{aligned}|=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 9 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 0.5 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0.2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 12 & 4\\end{aligned}|=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186345335 :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -3 & 8 \\\\ 9 & 5 & -3 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 6 & 7 & 0\\end{aligned}|=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -3 & 8 \\\\ 9 & 5 & -3 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 6 & 7 & 0\\end{aligned}|=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186345336 :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}-7 & -1 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -5 & -7\\end{aligned}|=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}-7 & -1 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -5 & -7\\end{aligned}|=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186345341 :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}|=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}|=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186345342 :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -7 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -8 & -9 & 4\\end{aligned}|=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -7 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -8 & -9 & 4\\end{aligned}|=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348054 :question/worked-solution "Every linear transformation in ${R}^{2}$ can be represented as a $2\\times 2$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $i$ and $j$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nFrom the diagram we see that \n${i}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ \nand \n${j}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is \n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Every linear transformation in ${R}^{2}$ can be represented as a $2\\times 2$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $i$ and $j$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nFrom the diagram we see that \n$$\n\\displaystyle {i}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ \nand \n$$\n\\displaystyle {j}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is \n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348075 :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{25}-\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{8}=1?$" "What is the center of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{25}-\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{8}=1?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348076 :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{7}-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}=1?$" "What is the center of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{7}-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}=1?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348082 :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3(0.6)^{n+1}$ ?" "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3(0.6)^{n+1}\n$$ ?"] [:db/cas 17592186348083 :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series  ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{3}{(-3)}^{n+1}$ ?" "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series  $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{3}{(-3)}^{n+1}\n$$ ?"] [:db/cas 17592186348084 :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series  ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4}(2)^{n}$ ?" "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series  $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4}(2)^{n}\n$$ ?"] [:db/cas 17592186348085 :question/problem "For which values of $k$ does the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{64}{{2}^{k}}\\right)}^{n}$ converge?" "For which values of $k$ does the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{64}{{2}^{k}}\\right)}^{n}\n$$ converge?"] [:db/cas 17592186348114 :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{3}=1?$" "What is the center of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{3}=1?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348116 :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}-\\frac{(x+7)^{2}}{6}=1?$" "What is the center of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}-\\frac{(x+7)^{2}}{6}=1?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348121 :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}9\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}9\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348137 :question/problem "$\\int 9{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 9{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348141 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int 16{x}^{3}{log}_{3}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 16{x}^{3}{log}_{3}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348142 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int 25{x}^{4}{log}_{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 25{x}^{4}{log}_{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348143 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int 9{x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 9{x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348144 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int 25{x}^{4}{log}_{5}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 25{x}^{4}{log}_{5}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348145 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(2x)}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(2x)}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348146 :question/problem "$\\int {log}_{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int {log}_{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348147 :question/problem "$\\int 2{log}_{3}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 2{log}_{3}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348150 :question/problem "$\\int 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348171 :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{3}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/f8/f8617da98ea9d9fb48b8fef588b624c6961a299233836fe433c1819b697ca0fb.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)$ by $0-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ units to the left. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{3}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/f8/f8617da98ea9d9fb48b8fef588b624c6961a299233836fe433c1819b697ca0fb.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)$ by $$\n\\displaystyle 0-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{12}\n$$ units to the left. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186348176 :question/problem "Calculate ${E}^{-1}$, where\n$E=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Calculate ${E}^{-1}$, where\n$$\n\\displaystyle E=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348198 :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}})=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}})=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348198 :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore,\n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}})=1.$" "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore,\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}})=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348199 :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\})=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\})=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348199 :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore,\n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\})=2.$" "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore,\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\})=2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348201 :question/problem "Determine the dimension of $\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}.$" "Determine the dimension of $$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348202 :question/problem "Determine the dimension of $\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\}.$" "Determine the dimension of $$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348203 :question/problem "Determine the dimension of $\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.$" "Determine the dimension of $$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348207 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\frac{7}{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{\\left(\\frac{7}{x}\\right)}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=\\frac{7}{x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\frac{1}{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=\\frac{2}{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2}{x}\\right)}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=\\frac{2}{x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=\\frac{7}{x}$; $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$; $y=\\frac{2}{x}$." "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\frac{7}{x},$ we get $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{\\left(\\frac{7}{x}\\right)}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=\\frac{7}{x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\frac{1}{x},$ we get $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=\\frac{2}{x},$ we get $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2}{x}\\right)}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=\\frac{2}{x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=\\frac{7}{x}$; $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$; $y=\\frac{2}{x}$."] [:db/cas 17592186348208 :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{n} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $A$ is **not** invertible.* $\\,\\times$\n\nBy the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ we must have that $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)=n.$* $\\,✓$ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{rank}(A)=\\text{dim(Col}(A))=n.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}.$* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{rank}(A)=n$; $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}$ only." "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{n} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $A$ is **not** invertible.* $\\,\\times$\n\nBy the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ we must have that $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)=n.$* $\\,✓$ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{rank}(A)=\\text{dim(Col}(A))=n.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}.$* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{rank}(A)=n$; $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186348223 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\text{Null}(A)=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\right\\}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Null}(A)=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\right\\}?\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186348238 :question/problem "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{2}(x+2)^{2}.$" "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{2}(x+2)^{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348243 :question/problem "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}-{x}^{3}}\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right).$" "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}-{x}^{3}}\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348247 :question/problem "Given that $D=x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x},$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\frac{1}{x}?$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle D=x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x},\n$$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\frac{1}{x}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186348254 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2y}=0$ for $y>0.$" "Find the general solution to the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2y}=0\n$$ for $y>0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186348255 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=7{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\\right.$" "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=7{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348256 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=e{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-2{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\\right.$" "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=e{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-2{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348257 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2){x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\\right.$" "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2){x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348276 :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to write $\\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}$ in the standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y\\left(\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y+{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is in standard form with  $P(x)=-2$ and $Q(x)={x}^{2}.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs." "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to write $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}\n$$ in the standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y\\left(\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y+{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is in standard form with  $P(x)=-2$ and $Q(x)={x}^{2}.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs."] [:db/cas 17592186348296 :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=x\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a function and the known \nsolutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$ only." "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=x\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a function and the known \nsolutions.\n\n- The function $$\n\\displaystyle y(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}\n$$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186348351 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =4-\\frac{3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{2} \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\n\nTo find more solutions, we subtract integer multiples of $\\pi $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-\\pi +x=-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-2\\pi +x=-2\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{2}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our solutions are $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\left(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\n$$" "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =4-\\frac{3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{2} \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\n\nTo find more solutions, we subtract integer multiples of $\\pi $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-\\pi +x=-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-2\\pi +x=-2\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{2}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our solutions are $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\left(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348352 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-5 & =-5-3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ 10\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we subtract integer multiples of $\\pi $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\n$$" "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-5 & =-5-3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ 10\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we subtract integer multiples of $\\pi $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348353 :question/problem "Let \n$T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x+y \\\\ 2y\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Let \n$$\n\\displaystyle T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x+y \\\\ 2y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186348358 :question/problem "Find the standard matrix that  represents  the  linear  transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2},$ where\n$T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ x-y-z\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the standard matrix that  represents  the  linear  transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2},$ where\n$$\n\\displaystyle T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ x-y-z\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348359 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2}{|x+1|\\sqrt{(x+1)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{2}{|x+1|\\sqrt{(x+1)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348361 :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2.$" "Find an eigenvector of \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186348362 :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =2$ and $\\lambda =-1$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" "Given that $\\lambda =2$ and $\\lambda =-1$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$"] [:db/cas 17592186348362 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{5}{2}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For $\\lambda =-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{5}{2}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For $\\lambda =-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348363 :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =1$ and $\\lambda =10$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" "Given that $\\lambda =1$ and $\\lambda =10$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$"] [:db/cas 17592186348363 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}-1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For $\\lambda =10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}-10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 6 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}-1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For $\\lambda =10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}-10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 6 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348367 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\sqrt{1-z}}.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\sqrt{1-z}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348368 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{8-{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{8-{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348381 :question/problem "Determine if the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" "Determine if the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$"] [:db/cas 17592186348382 :question/problem "Determine if the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" "Determine if the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$"] [:db/cas 17592186348383 :question/problem "Determine if the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -12 \\\\ 4 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$ \nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" "Determine if the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -12 \\\\ 4 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ \nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$"] [:db/cas 17592186348396 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x+2y}{4}?$" "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x+2y}{4}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348401 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}\\frac{1}{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A$ over the rectangle $R=[1,e]\\times [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" "Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}\\frac{1}{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ over the rectangle $R=[1,e]\\times [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$"] [:db/cas 17592186348402 :question/problem "Find the standard matrix that represents  the  linear  transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{4},$ where\n$T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}7x-3y \\\\ x-2y+z \\\\ 0 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the standard matrix that represents  the  linear  transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{4},$ where\n$$\n\\displaystyle T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}7x-3y \\\\ x-2y+z \\\\ 0 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348403 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}\\frac{{e}^{y}}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$ over the rectangle $R=[1,\\sqrt{3}]\\times [0,1].$" "Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}\\frac{{e}^{y}}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ over the rectangle $R=[1,\\sqrt{3}]\\times [0,1].$"] [:db/cas 17592186348405 :question/worked-solution "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} &=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-10{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -10 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & -1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -10 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=3,$ we get the vector \n$\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=5,$ $b=4,$ and $c=3.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a+c}{b}=\\frac{5+3}{4}=2.$" "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} &=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-10{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -10 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & -1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -10 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=3,$ we get the vector \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=5,$ $b=4,$ and $c=3.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a+c}{b}=\\frac{5+3}{4}=2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186348406 :question/worked-solution "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} &=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -8 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=2,$ we get the vector \n$\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=-5,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=2.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{c}{a+b}=\\frac{2}{-5+(-1)}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$" "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} &=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -8 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=2,$ we get the vector \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=-5,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=2.$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{c}{a+b}=\\frac{2}{-5+(-1)}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348407 :question/worked-solution "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} &=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{3} \\\\ -2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=1$, we get the vector \n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=1,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=1.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a+b}{c}=\\frac{1+(-2)}{1}=-1.$" "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} &=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{3} \\\\ -2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=1$, we get the vector \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=1,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=1.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a+b}{c}=\\frac{1+(-2)}{1}=-1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186348408 :question/worked-solution "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} &=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=4$, we get the vector \n$\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=-10,$ $b=-3,$ and $c=4.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a+c}=\\frac{-3}{-10+4}=\\frac{1}{2}.$" "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} &=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=4$, we get the vector \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=-10,$ $b=-3,$ and $c=4.$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{b}{a+c}=\\frac{-3}{-10+4}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348409 :question/worked-solution "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} &=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=5$, we get the vector \n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=-1,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=5.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b+c}{a}=\\frac{-2+5}{-1}=-3.$" "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} &=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=5$, we get the vector \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=-1,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=5.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b+c}{a}=\\frac{-2+5}{-1}=-3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186348410 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/d2/d2b5c55b15f594680e8f299899d690cd1d92baa1ebef31ce5c79af52afec631e.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\frac{2x+y}{y}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{2x+y}{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function $\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ \\frac{2x+y}{y}, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}$. Since $D∖R$ is empty, the function $f(x,y)$ will be identical to zero for all points in $R,$ including $D.$ But $\\frac{2x+y}{y}$ is not zero, for example, at $(x,y)=(0,2)\\in D.$\n\n- Functions $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{y}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$ and $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$ equals $\\frac{2x+y}{y}$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{y}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$; $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$ only." "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/d2/d2b5c55b15f594680e8f299899d690cd1d92baa1ebef31ce5c79af52afec631e.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\frac{2x+y}{y}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{2x+y}{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ \\frac{2x+y}{y}, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}.\n$$ Since $D∖R$ is empty, the function $f(x,y)$ will be identical to zero for all points in $R,$ including $D.$ But $\\frac{2x+y}{y}$ is not zero, for example, at $(x,y)=(0,2)\\in D.$\n\n- Functions $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{y}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\n$$ equals $\\frac{2x+y}{y}$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{y}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned};\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186348412 :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “${∬}_{R}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[0,2]\\times [-1,1]$” is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$ Also, the integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement “${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[0,2]\\times [-2,2]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement “${∬}_{R}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[0,3]\\times [-2,2]$” is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[0,2]\\times [-2,2]$” only." "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[0,2]\\times [-1,1]$” is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$ Also, the integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[0,2]\\times [-2,2]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[0,3]\\times [-2,2]$” is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[0,2]\\times [-2,2]$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186348413 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/a7/a7af801fa9975dda6ce3b12b2cde2c328721cd5e0affd16f1a14ab26cfa21bdd.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}(3x-y), & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statements “${∬}_{D}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-3,2]\\times [-4,1]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement “${∬}_{R}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-4,4]$” is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statements “${∬}_{D}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-4,0]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${∬}_{D}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-3,2]\\times [-4,1]$”; “${∬}_{D}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-4,0]$” only." "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/a7/a7af801fa9975dda6ce3b12b2cde2c328721cd5e0affd16f1a14ab26cfa21bdd.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}(3x-y), & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statements “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-3,2]\\times [-4,1]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-4,4]$” is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statements “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-4,0]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-3,2]\\times [-4,1]$”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-4,0]$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186348418 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-2$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-1+2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3+2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}-{w}_{2} &=1 \\\\ {w}_{1}-{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is the same as the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}={w}_{2}+1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get ${w}_{1}=1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=1,b=1,$ and $a+b=2.$" "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-2$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-1+2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3+2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}-{w}_{2} &=1 \\\\ {w}_{1}-{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is the same as the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}={w}_{2}+1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get ${w}_{1}=1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=1,b=1,$ and $a+b=2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186348419 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-1$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1+1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -3+1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{w}_{1}+4{w}_{2} &=-2 \\\\ -1{w}_{1}-2{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-1-2{w}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get ${w}_{1}=-1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=-2,b=-1,$ and $a+b=-3.$" "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-1$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1+1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -3+1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{w}_{1}+4{w}_{2} &=-2 \\\\ -1{w}_{1}-2{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-1-2{w}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get ${w}_{1}=-1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=-2,b=-1,$ and $a+b=-3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186348421 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3-1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -1-1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{w}_{1}+4{w}_{2} &=-2 \\\\ -{w}_{1}-2{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-1-2{w}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get ${w}_{1}=-1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}-(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(2t-1){e}^{t} \\\\ (1-t){e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3-1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -1-1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{w}_{1}+4{w}_{2} &=-2 \\\\ -{w}_{1}-2{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-1-2{w}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get ${w}_{1}=-1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}-(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(2t-1){e}^{t} \\\\ (1-t){e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348423 :question/problem "Calculate ${∬}_{R}(2+2x-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$,  where $R=[0,3]\\times [0,2].$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}(2+2x-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$  where $R=[0,3]\\times [0,2].$"] [:db/cas 17592186348424 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}\\frac{2+6y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$,  where $R=[1,2]\\times [2,4].$" "Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}\\frac{2+6y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$  where $R=[1,2]\\times [2,4].$"] [:db/cas 17592186348426 :question/problem "![](images/a5/a51b5dca6436e6ffd3af0d1cfa801141d97e7660e6218344011a5ac965c722c6.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=2(x-2)^{2}-1$ and $y=2-(x-2)^{2},$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" "![](images/a5/a51b5dca6436e6ffd3af0d1cfa801141d97e7660e6218344011a5ac965c722c6.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=2(x-2)^{2}-1$ and $y=2-(x-2)^{2},$ as shown above, then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348427 :question/problem "Calculate ${∬}_{R}(6{x}^{2}-10{y}^{4})\\text{d}A$,  where $R=[-2,0]\\times [-1,1].$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}(6{x}^{2}-10{y}^{4})\\text{d}A,\n$$  where $R=[-2,0]\\times [-1,1].$"] [:db/cas 17592186348440 :question/problem "Given the linear transformation $T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2x-y+3z \\\\ 8x-3y+10z \\\\ -6x+3y-9z\\end{bmatrix},$ find $\\text{nullity}(T).$" "Given the linear transformation $$\n\\displaystyle T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2x-y+3z \\\\ 8x-3y+10z \\\\ -6x+3y-9z\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $\\text{nullity}(T).$"] [:db/cas 17592186348450 :question/worked-solution "Let's define  $f(x,y,z)={2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=2.$  \n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(1,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy})j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy})k \\\\  & =({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\cdot 2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)i+({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\cdot 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)j+({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\cdot 6z\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)k \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(y\\,\\text{ }{2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\text{ }i+x\\,\\text{ }{2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\text{ }j+3z\\,\\text{ }{2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\text{ }k) \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,1) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2((-1)\\cdot \\text{ }{2}^{3(1)^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)}\\text{ }i+1\\cdot \\,\\text{ }{2}^{3(1)^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)}\\text{ }j+3(1)\\cdot {2}^{3(1)^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)}\\text{ }k) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(-{2}^{1}\\text{ }i+{2}^{1}\\text{ }j+3\\cdot {2}^{1}\\text{ }k) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(-i+j+3\\text{ }k) \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩=⟨-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{c}{b}=\\frac{12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}=3.$" "Let's define  $f(x,y,z)={2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=2.$  \n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(1,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy})j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy})k \\\\  & =({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\cdot 2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)i+({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\cdot 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)j+({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\cdot 6z\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)k \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(y\\,\\text{ }{2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\text{ }i+x\\,\\text{ }{2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\text{ }j+3z\\,\\text{ }{2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\text{ }k) \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,1) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2((-1)\\cdot \\text{ }{2}^{3(1)^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)}\\text{ }i+1\\cdot \\,\\text{ }{2}^{3(1)^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)}\\text{ }j+3(1)\\cdot {2}^{3(1)^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)}\\text{ }k) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(-{2}^{1}\\text{ }i+{2}^{1}\\text{ }j+3\\cdot {2}^{1}\\text{ }k) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(-i+j+3\\text{ }k) \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩=⟨-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{c}{b}=\\frac{12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}=3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351178 :question/problem "What linear transformation $T$ is represented by the following matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}?$" "What linear transformation $T$ is represented by the following matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351183 :question/problem "What linear transformation $T$ is represented by the following matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}?$" "What linear transformation $T$ is represented by the following matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351186 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3+3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3+3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+5{w}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=\\frac{1}{5},$ and $a+b=\\frac{1}{5}.$" "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3+3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3+3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+5{w}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=\\frac{1}{5},$ and $a+b=\\frac{1}{5}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186351187 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}7-5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3-5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{w}_{1}-2{w}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 2{w}_{1}-2{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is the same as the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}={w}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=1,b=\\frac{1}{2},$ and $a+b=\\frac{3}{2}.$" "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}7-5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3-5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{w}_{1}-2{w}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 2{w}_{1}-2{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is the same as the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}={w}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=1,b=\\frac{1}{2},$ and $a+b=\\frac{3}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186351188 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-2$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2+2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2+2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 5{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{5} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{5} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}. \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ (2+5t){e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-2$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2+2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2+2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 5{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{5} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{5} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}. \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ (2+5t){e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351189 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=\\sqrt{x}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,1),$ then  ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+y)\\,dx+(3x+2y)\\,dy=$" "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=\\sqrt{x}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,1),$ then  $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+y)\\,dx+(3x+2y)\\,dy=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351196 :question/problem "Given that \n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2(x-1){y}^{2} & 2(x-1)^{2}y\\end{bmatrix},$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(0,1).$" "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2(x-1){y}^{2} & 2(x-1)^{2}y\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(0,1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186351199 :question/problem "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2+\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2+\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186351199 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-2+\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2+\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3-\\text{i} & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3-\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(-3-\\text{i}){x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+(3-\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-2+\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5}(3-\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{5}(3-\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-5,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}3-\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-5}{3-\\text{i}}=\\frac{-5(3+\\text{i})}{10}=-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}.$" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-2+\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2+\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3-\\text{i} & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3-\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(-3-\\text{i}){x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+(3-\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-2+\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5}(3-\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{5}(3-\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-5,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}3-\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-5}{3-\\text{i}}=\\frac{-5(3+\\text{i})}{10}=-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351201 :question/problem "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 13 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1+3\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 13 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1+3\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186351201 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-1+3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 13 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1+3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2-3\\text{i} & -1 \\\\ 13 & 2-3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(-2-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 13{x}_{1}+(2-3\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-1+3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-2-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ -(2+3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2+3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-1}{2+3\\text{i}}=\\frac{-(2-3\\text{i})}{13}=-\\frac{2}{13}+\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}.$" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-1+3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 13 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1+3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2-3\\text{i} & -1 \\\\ 13 & 2-3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(-2-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 13{x}_{1}+(2-3\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-1+3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-2-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ -(2+3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2+3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-1}{2+3\\text{i}}=\\frac{-(2-3\\text{i})}{13}=-\\frac{2}{13}+\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351202 :question/problem "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186351202 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i} & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+(-2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}(2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}(2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-2}{2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}=\\frac{-2(2-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i})}{6}=-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\text{i}.$" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i} & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+(-2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}(2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}(2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-2}{2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}=\\frac{-2(2-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i})}{6}=-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\text{i}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351206 :question/problem "Given that ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{bmatrix},$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}?$" "Given that ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186351215 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}-y\\,dx+x\\,dy,$ where $C$ is a portion of the circle parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}-y\\,dx+x\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is a portion of the circle parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$"] [:db/cas 17592186351216 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y={x}^{2}-x$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(2,2),$ then ${\\int }_{C}2xy\\,dx+x\\,dy=$" "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y={x}^{2}-x$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(2,2),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}2xy\\,dx+x\\,dy=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351217 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-5y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda -5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +5)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-5$ or $\\lambda =1$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$ Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x \\\\ {y}^{″} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+4(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)-5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n(-14\\alpha +12\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+(-14\\beta -12\\alpha )\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-14\\alpha +12\\beta &=1\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x) \\\\ -14\\beta -12\\alpha &=-4\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{10},\\beta =\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nNow let us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-\\frac{2}{5}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{10}.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-5A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{x}-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{10}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n-5A+B=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A+B &=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -5A+B &=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=0,$ $B=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$" "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-5y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda -5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +5)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-5$ or $\\lambda =1$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$ Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x \\\\ {y}^{″} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+4(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)-5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n(-14\\alpha +12\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+(-14\\beta -12\\alpha )\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-14\\alpha +12\\beta &=1\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x) \\\\ -14\\beta -12\\alpha &=-4\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\alpha =\\frac{1}{10},\\beta =\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nNow let us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-\\frac{2}{5}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{10}.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-5A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{x}-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{10}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n-5A+B=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A+B &=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -5A+B &=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=0,$ $B=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351218 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(2,3),$ then ${\\int }_{C}{e}^{x}\\,dx-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{2}\\right)\\,dy=$" "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(2,3),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{e}^{x}\\,dx-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{2}\\right)\\,dy=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351222 :question/problem "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ of ${R}^{2},$ find $[x]_{B}$ if $x=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Given the **orthonormal** basis $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ of ${R}^{2},$ find $[x]_{B}$ if $x=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186351223 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n$\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3}\\},$ \n$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\},$ and \n$\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{2}\\},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} &=1 \\\\ {u}_{3}\\cdot {u}_{3} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\n- $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} &=1 \\\\ {w}_{3}\\cdot {w}_{3} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=1+25+4=30\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $U$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}=-\\frac{6}{13}+\\frac{6}{13}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{4}{13}+\\frac{9}{13}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{9}{13}+\\frac{4}{13}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $V$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{18}}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{18}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{18}}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{4}{6}+\\frac{1}{6}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthonormal.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$V$ and $W$ only\"." "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n$\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3}\\},$ \n$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\},$ and \n$\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{2}\\},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} &=1 \\\\ {u}_{3}\\cdot {u}_{3} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} &=1 \\\\ {w}_{3}\\cdot {w}_{3} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=1+25+4=30\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $U$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}=-\\frac{6}{13}+\\frac{6}{13}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{4}{13}+\\frac{9}{13}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{9}{13}+\\frac{4}{13}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $V$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{18}}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{18}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{18}}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{4}{6}+\\frac{1}{6}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthonormal.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$V$ and $W$ only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186351224 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{″}(x,y)$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}e-{e}^{2-x} & 2y\\end{bmatrix}$" "Find ${f}^{″}(x,y)$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}e-{e}^{2-x} & 2y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351226 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vector of $U$ as ${u}_{1}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$, we need to find a second vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\nFrom the above equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we obtain the vector \n$x=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|=|-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\sqrt{2}.$" "Let's denote the vector of $U$ as ${u}_{1}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$, we need to find a second vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\nFrom the above equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we obtain the vector \n$$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle |{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|=|-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351227 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vector of $U$ as ${u}_{1}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$, we need to find a second vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0\\,⟹\\,\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\nFrom the above equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we obtain the vector \n$x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}=\\sqrt{5}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|=|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}|+|-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}|=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}.$" "Let's denote the vector of $U$ as ${u}_{1}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$, we need to find a second vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0\\,⟹\\,\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\nFrom the above equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we obtain the vector \n$$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}=\\sqrt{5}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle |{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|=|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}|+|-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}|=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351228 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x &=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\sqrt{10}\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=\\sqrt{14}\\cdot {R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=3{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-2{x}_{1}+3(3{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=5{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5{x}_{3} \\\\ 3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we obtain the vector \n$x=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{35}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{35}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{35}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{35}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{35}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{35}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{35}}\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|=|\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{35}}|+|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{35}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{35}}|=\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{35}}.$" "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x &=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\sqrt{10}\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=\\sqrt{14}\\cdot {R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=3{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-2{x}_{1}+3(3{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=5{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5{x}_{3} \\\\ 3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we obtain the vector \n$$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{35}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{35}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{35}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{35}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{35}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{35}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{35}}\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle |{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|=|\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{35}}|+|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{35}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{35}}|=\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{35}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351246 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{(2x+y+1)}{x}\\,dx+dy,$ where $C$ is a portion of the parabola parametrized by $r(t)=t\\,i+({t}^{2}-1)\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2].$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{(2x+y+1)}{x}\\,dx+dy,\n$$ where $C$ is a portion of the parabola parametrized by $r(t)=t\\,i+({t}^{2}-1)\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2].$"] [:db/cas 17592186351249 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy,$ where $C$ is a portion of the ellipse parametrized by $r(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is a portion of the ellipse parametrized by $r(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$"] [:db/cas 17592186351250 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y=\\pi {x}^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(1,\\pi )$ to point $B(2,4\\pi ),$ then ${\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)\\,dx+3x\\,dy=$" "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y=\\pi {x}^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(1,\\pi )$ to point $B(2,4\\pi ),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)\\,dx+3x\\,dy=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351251 :question/problem "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{5}\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{5}\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186351251 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =\\sqrt{5}\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5}\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i} & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+(-2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}(2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}(2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-3}{2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}}=\\frac{-3(2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i})}{9}=-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\text{i}.$" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =\\sqrt{5}\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5}\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i} & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+(-2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}(2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}(2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-3}{2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}}=\\frac{-3(2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i})}{9}=-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\text{i}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351252 :question/problem "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2-3\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2-3\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186351252 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =2-3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2-3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2-3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\text{i} & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+3\\text{i}{x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(2-3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\text{i}{x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{\\text{i}}{1}=\\text{i}.$" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =2-3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2-3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2-3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\text{i} & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+3\\text{i}{x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(2-3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\text{i}{x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{\\text{i}}{1}=\\text{i}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351253 :question/problem "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2+3\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2+3\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186351253 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =2+3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2+3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-3\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -2 & -1-3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+(-1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(2+3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}(1+3\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}(1+3\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=-\\frac{1+3\\text{i}}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}.$" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =2+3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2+3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-3\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -2 & -1-3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+(-1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(2+3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}(1+3\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}(1+3\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{a}{b}=-\\frac{1+3\\text{i}}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351254 :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}.$" "Let ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186351255 :question/problem "Given that ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix},$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}?$" "Given that ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186351261 :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote \n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-2)\\cdot 1+5\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{2}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{11}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{11}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, we have $a=-\\frac{4}{3},$ $c=\\frac{13}{3},$ and \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{13}{3}\\right)}=-\\frac{4}{13}.\n$$" "First, let's denote \n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-2)\\cdot 1+5\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{2}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{11}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{11}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, we have $a=-\\frac{4}{3},$ $c=\\frac{13}{3},$ and \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{13}{3}\\right)}=-\\frac{4}{13}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351263 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 1+5\\cdot (-1)+6\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $b=6,$ $c=5,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{6}{5}.\n$$" "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 1+5\\cdot (-1)+6\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $b=6,$ $c=5,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{6}{5}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351265 :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{5\\cdot 4+(-4)\\cdot 0+10\\cdot 3}{{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{4}^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{41}}{41} \\\\ -\\frac{4\\sqrt{41}}{41} \\\\ \\frac{4\\sqrt{41}}{41}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{41}}{41} \\\\ -\\frac{4\\sqrt{41}}{41} \\\\ \\frac{4\\sqrt{41}}{41}\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is an orthonormal basis of $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{41}}{41},$ $b=-\\frac{4\\sqrt{41}}{41},$ $c=\\frac{4\\sqrt{41}}{41}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|-\\frac{3\\sqrt{41}}{41}|+|-\\frac{4\\sqrt{41}}{41}|+|\\frac{4\\sqrt{41}}{41}|=\\frac{11\\sqrt{41}}{41}.\n$$" "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{5\\cdot 4+(-4)\\cdot 0+10\\cdot 3}{{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{4}^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{41}}{41} \\\\ -\\frac{4\\sqrt{41}}{41} \\\\ \\frac{4\\sqrt{41}}{41}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{41}}{41} \\\\ -\\frac{4\\sqrt{41}}{41} \\\\ \\frac{4\\sqrt{41}}{41}\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is an orthonormal basis of $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{41}}{41},$ $b=-\\frac{4\\sqrt{41}}{41},$ $c=\\frac{4\\sqrt{41}}{41}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|-\\frac{3\\sqrt{41}}{41}|+|-\\frac{4\\sqrt{41}}{41}|+|\\frac{4\\sqrt{41}}{41}|=\\frac{11\\sqrt{41}}{41}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351271 :question/problem "![](images/51/5197029ed6803a39bf6d6d14bbdd8b3a71d8905e854bf3842305828280cbc39b.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2},\\,$ $x=6-{y}^{2},$ and $y=0,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" "![](images/51/5197029ed6803a39bf6d6d14bbdd8b3a71d8905e854bf3842305828280cbc39b.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2},\\,$ $x=6-{y}^{2},$ and $y=0,$ as shown above, then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351272 :question/problem "![](images/5d/5df807dc424366c043211f7d3a1667bc2c9888f24292856ed99a07d7ac586a94.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $x=3-(y-3)^{2},\\,$ $x=(y-3)^{2}+4,$ $y=0,$ and $y=3,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" "![](image