splaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}{x}_{i}^{2}=440,\n$$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 440 \\\\  & =\\frac{440}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{110}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{110}{3}-{(-5)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{110}{3}-25 \\\\  & =\\frac{110-75}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354632 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-x)^{2}) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x+1) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1-x) \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{3}-2x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{3}-2(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+2x \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}-2x) \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\int }_{-5}^{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x$; ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x$ only." "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-x)^{2}) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x+1) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1-x) \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{3}-2x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{3}-2(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+2x \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}-2x) \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-5}^{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186354635 :question/problem "Given that $B=\\begin{bmatrix}6\\sqrt{2} & -5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix},$ find $det(B).$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}6\\sqrt{2} & -5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $det(B).$"] [:db/cas 17592186354636 :question/problem "Given that \n$|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|=4,$\nfind \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|.$" "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|=4,\n$$\nfind \n$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}a+1 & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354637 :question/problem "Given that \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b+3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=5,$\nfind \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|.$" "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}a+2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b+3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=5,\n$$\nfind \n$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}a+1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354639 :question/problem "Find\n$|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 91 & 92 & 93 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{aligned}|.$" "Find\n$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 91 & 92 & 93 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{aligned}|.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354640 :question/problem "Find\n$|\\begin{aligned}97 & 98 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{aligned}|.$" "Find\n$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}97 & 98 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{aligned}|.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354641 :question/worked-solution "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{5\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ 2\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-5 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-5 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =5 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)=1.190\\,289\\,949…≈1.190\\,290\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places. \n\nSince $1.190\\,290$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=1.190\\,290.$\n\nTo find the other solution, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\pi +1.190\\,289\\,949…=4.331\\,882\\,603\\,…≈4.331\\,883\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places. \n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈1.190\\,290\\cdot 4.331\\,883≈5.156\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{5\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ 2\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-5 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-5 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =5 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)=1.190\\,289\\,949…≈1.190\\,290\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places. \n\nSince $1.190\\,290$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=1.190\\,290.$\n\nTo find the other solution, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\pi +1.190\\,289\\,949…=4.331\\,882\\,603\\,…≈4.331\\,883\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places. \n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈1.190\\,290\\cdot 4.331\\,883≈5.156\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186354643 :question/worked-solution "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{4\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+4 & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)=-0.927\\,295\\,218…≈-0.927\\,295\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to 6 decimal places. \n\nSince $-0.927\\,295$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\nTo generate the solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\pi +x=-0.927\\,295…+\\pi ≈2.214\\,297 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =2\\pi +x=-0.927\\,295…+2\\pi ≈5.355\\,890\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈2.214\\,297\\cdot 5.355\\,890≈11.860\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{4\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+4 & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)=-0.927\\,295\\,218…≈-0.927\\,295\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to 6 decimal places. \n\nSince $-0.927\\,295$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\nTo generate the solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\pi +x=-0.927\\,295…+\\pi ≈2.214\\,297 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =2\\pi +x=-0.927\\,295…+2\\pi ≈5.355\\,890\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈2.214\\,297\\cdot 5.355\\,890≈11.860\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186354647 :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x\\cdot {6}^{x-2} & =7\\cdot {6}^{x-2}-3{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{x-2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{x-2}+4x\\cdot {6}^{x-2}-7\\cdot {6}^{x-2} & =0 \\\\ (3{x}^{2}+4x-7)\\cdot {6}^{x-2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+4x-7=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{6}^{x-2}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $3{x}^{2}+4x-7=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(3x+7)(x-1)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\frac{7}{3},1.$\n\n- The equation ${6}^{x-2}=0$ has no solutions because ${6}^{x-2}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-\\frac{7}{3},1,$ and their product is $\\left(-\\frac{7}{3}\\right)\\cdot 1=-\\frac{7}{3}.$" "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x\\cdot {6}^{x-2} & =7\\cdot {6}^{x-2}-3{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{x-2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{x-2}+4x\\cdot {6}^{x-2}-7\\cdot {6}^{x-2} & =0 \\\\ (3{x}^{2}+4x-7)\\cdot {6}^{x-2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+4x-7=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{6}^{x-2}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $3{x}^{2}+4x-7=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(3x+7)(x-1)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\frac{7}{3},1.$\n\n- The equation ${6}^{x-2}=0$ has no solutions because ${6}^{x-2}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-\\frac{7}{3},1,$ and their product is $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(-\\frac{7}{3}\\right)\\cdot 1=-\\frac{7}{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354650 :question/problem "Simplify the integral ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry." "Simplify the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{1}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ using symmetry."] [:db/cas 17592186354651 :question/problem "${\\int }_{-5}^{5}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-5}^{5}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354654 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(-x)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is an odd function, but integral ${\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $h(x)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$ only." "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(-x)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is an odd function, but integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is not of the form $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-a}^{a}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $h(x)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186354655 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(-x)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-4x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)({e}^{(-x)}+{e}^{-(-x)}) \\\\  & =-x({e}^{-x}+{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(-x)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\  & \\ne -h(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x$; ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x$ only." "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(-x)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-4x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)({e}^{(-x)}+{e}^{-(-x)}) \\\\  & =-x({e}^{-x}+{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(-x)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\  & \\ne -h(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{2}x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186354656 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x){e}^{-(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =-x{e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-7}^{7}x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{x}$  is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)^{2}{e}^{(-x)} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{e}^{-x} \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=(x+1)^{3}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =((-x)+1)^{3} \\\\  & =(1-x)^{3} \\\\  & \\ne -h(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\int }_{-7}^{7}x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$ only." "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x){e}^{-(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =-x{e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-7}^{7}x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{x}$  is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)^{2}{e}^{(-x)} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{e}^{-x} \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=(x+1)^{3}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =((-x)+1)^{3} \\\\  & =(1-x)^{3} \\\\  & \\ne -h(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-7}^{7}x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186357357 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{3}-2$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{3}-2 \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}-2 \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}+2) \\\\  & \\ne -h(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{5}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{5}+(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{5}-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-({x}^{5}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{5}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}})$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{(-x)^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{5}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x$ only." "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{3}-2$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{3}-2 \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}-2 \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}+2) \\\\  & \\ne -h(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{5}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{5}+(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{5}-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-({x}^{5}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{5}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}})$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{(-x)^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{5}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186357358 :question/worked-solution "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{16\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ 4\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-16 & =0 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-16 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =16 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{16}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4)=1.325\\,817\\,663…≈1.325\\,818\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places. \n\nSince $1.325\\,818$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=1.325\\,818.$\n\nTo find the other solution, we subtract $\\pi $ from the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=1.325\\,817\\,663…-\\pi =-1.815\\,774\\,989…≈-1.815\\,775\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈1.325\\,818\\cdot (-1.815\\,775)≈-2.407\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{16\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ 4\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-16 & =0 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-16 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =16 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{16}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4)=1.325\\,817\\,663…≈1.325\\,818\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places. \n\nSince $1.325\\,818$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=1.325\\,818.$\n\nTo find the other solution, we subtract $\\pi $ from the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=1.325\\,817\\,663…-\\pi =-1.815\\,774\\,989…≈-1.815\\,775\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈1.325\\,818\\cdot (-1.815\\,775)≈-2.407\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186357361 :question/worked-solution "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{4\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ 8\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+4 & =0 \\\\ 8\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+4 & =0 \\\\ 8\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-0.463\\,647…≈-0.463\\,65\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places.\n\nSince $-0.463\\,65$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-0.463\\,65.$\n\nTo find the other solution, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-0.463\\,65…+\\pi ≈2.677\\,95\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈-0.463\\,65\\cdot 2.677\\,95≈-1.24\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{4\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ 8\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+4 & =0 \\\\ 8\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+4 & =0 \\\\ 8\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-0.463\\,647…≈-0.463\\,65\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places.\n\nSince $-0.463\\,65$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-0.463\\,65.$\n\nTo find the other solution, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-0.463\\,65…+\\pi ≈2.677\\,95\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈-0.463\\,65\\cdot 2.677\\,95≈-1.24\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186357395 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-14 & 12 & 3 \\\\ -20 & 17 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 12 & 3 \\\\ -20 & 15 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-16{x}_{1}+12{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -20{x}_{1}+15{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 12 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -20 & 15 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{5}{4}\\right){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 12 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 12 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-14 & 12 & 3 \\\\ -20 & 17 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 12 & 3 \\\\ -20 & 15 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-16{x}_{1}+12{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -20{x}_{1}+15{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 12 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -20 & 15 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{5}{4}\\right){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 12 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 12 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector\n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357422 :question/worked-solution "First, we write $F(s)$ as a sum of partial fractions, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}-9}=\\frac{1}{(s-3)(s+3)}=\\frac{1}{6(s-3)}-\\frac{1}{6(s+3)}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{3t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-3},\\,s>3 \\\\ L{{e}^{-3t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+3},\\,s>-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the addition property of the Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{6(s-3)}-\\frac{1}{6(s+3)}} & =\\frac{1}{6}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-3}}-\\frac{1}{6}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{3t}-\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the inverse Laplace transform of $F(s)=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}-9}$ is $f(t)=\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{3t}-\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{-3t}.$" "First, we write $F(s)$ as a sum of partial fractions, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}-9}=\\frac{1}{(s-3)(s+3)}=\\frac{1}{6(s-3)}-\\frac{1}{6(s+3)}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{3t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-3},\\,s>3 \\\\ L{{e}^{-3t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+3},\\,s>-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the addition property of the Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{6(s-3)}-\\frac{1}{6(s+3)}} & =\\frac{1}{6}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-3}}-\\frac{1}{6}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{3t}-\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the inverse Laplace transform of $F(s)=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}-9}$ is $$\n\\displaystyle f(t)=\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{3t}-\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{-3t}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357450 :question/problem "$\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357457 :question/problem "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x+3y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2y}\\right).$" "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y)=\\frac{x+3y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2y}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357458 :question/problem "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)=\\frac{4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3y}{x}\\right).$" "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $$\n\\displaystyle g(x,y)=\\frac{4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3y}{x}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357459 :question/worked-solution "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is NOT the case is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}.$\n\nWe see that it is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree $2.$\n\nHence, the original differential equation is  NOT homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\,\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is not homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)\\ne f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(tx,ty)\\ne f(x,y),$ the original differential equation is NOT homogeneous." "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is NOT the case is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe see that it is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree $2.$\n\nHence, the original differential equation is  NOT homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\,\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is not homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)\\ne f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(tx,ty)\\ne f(x,y),$ the original differential equation is NOT homogeneous."] [:db/cas 17592186357486 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}-\\frac{1}{z}\\,dx+x\\,dy+dz,$ where $C$ is a portion of the helix parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}-\\frac{1}{z}\\,dx+x\\,dy+dz,\n$$ where $C$ is a portion of the helix parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$"] [:db/cas 17592186357487 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y={x}^{2}-1$ traversed from the point $A(0,-1)$ to point $B(2,3),$ then ${\\int }_{C}5{x}^{2}y\\,dx+{x}^{2}(y+1)\\,dy=$" "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y={x}^{2}-1$ traversed from the point $A(0,-1)$ to point $B(2,3),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}5{x}^{2}y\\,dx+{x}^{2}(y+1)\\,dy=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357488 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y=\\pi {x}^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,\\pi ),$ then ${\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\right)\\,dx+4{x}^{2}\\,dy=$" "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y=\\pi {x}^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,\\pi ),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\right)\\,dx+4{x}^{2}\\,dy=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357496 :question/problem "Given that $D=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x},$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x?$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle D=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x},\n$$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x?$"] [:db/cas 17592186357497 :question/problem "Given that $D={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x},$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x?$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle D={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x},\n$$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x?$"] [:db/cas 17592186357498 :question/problem "Given that $D=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-3\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x},$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x?$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle D=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-3\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x},\n$$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x?$"] [:db/cas 17592186357499 :question/problem "Given that $D={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x},$ what is $D(y)$ for $y={x}^{4}?$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle D={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x},\n$$ what is $D(y)$ for $y={x}^{4}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186357514 :question/problem "Calculate the value of \n$\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}|.$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the right of ${a}_{21}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*" "Calculate the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the right of ${a}_{21}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*"] [:db/cas 17592186357515 :question/problem "Calculate the value of \n$\\,|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|.$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left of ${a}_{44}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*" "Calculate the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left of ${a}_{44}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*"] [:db/cas 17592186357549 :question/problem "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix \n$Q=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle Q=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357550 :question/problem "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$L=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle L=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357562 :question/worked-solution "А second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n2{y}^{2}{y}^{″}-{e}^{x}{y}^{′}=x\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $2{y}^{2}{y}^{″}.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=\\frac{1}{x},b(x)=1,c(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ and $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+17y=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=1,b(x)=0,c(x)=17,$ and $f(x)=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$; ${y}^{″}+17y=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ only." "А second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n2{y}^{2}{y}^{″}-{e}^{x}{y}^{′}=x\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $2{y}^{2}{y}^{″}.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=\\frac{1}{x},b(x)=1,c(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ and $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+17y=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=1,b(x)=0,c(x)=17,$ and $f(x)=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{x}{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x;\n$$ ${y}^{″}+17y=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186357565 :question/problem "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =8,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =8,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186357567 :question/problem "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186357576 :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\lambda =4$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" "Given that $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\lambda =4$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$"] [:db/cas 17592186357576 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-(-3)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For $\\lambda =4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-(-3)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For $\\lambda =4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get the eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357584 :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda =10$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" "Given that $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda =10$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$"] [:db/cas 17592186357584 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For $\\lambda =10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}-10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-8{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For $\\lambda =10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}-10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-8{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357607 :question/worked-solution "We are given that \n\n$\\,$ $x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nSubstituting these into the bilinear expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & =2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-4{x}_{2}{y}_{3}+{x}_{3}{y}_{1}-3{x}_{3}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 0-4\\cdot 2\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot 1-3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0-24-1-0 \\\\  & =-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We are given that \n\n$\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \n\nSubstituting these into the bilinear expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & =2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-4{x}_{2}{y}_{3}+{x}_{3}{y}_{1}-3{x}_{3}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 0-4\\cdot 2\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot 1-3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0-24-1-0 \\\\  & =-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357642 :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is  $2{x}^{2}+1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is of the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are the constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation and group the terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(2\\alpha )+2x(2\\alpha x)-12(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta ) & =2{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ (2\\alpha +4\\alpha -12\\alpha ){x}^{2}-12\\beta  & =2{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ -6\\alpha {x}^{2}-12\\beta  & =2{x}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha &=2\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ -12\\beta &=1\\, && (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\,\\beta =-\\frac{1}{12}.$\n\nHence, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{1}{12}.\n$$" "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is  $2{x}^{2}+1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is of the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are the constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation and group the terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(2\\alpha )+2x(2\\alpha x)-12(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta ) & =2{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ (2\\alpha +4\\alpha -12\\alpha ){x}^{2}-12\\beta  & =2{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ -6\\alpha {x}^{2}-12\\beta  & =2{x}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha &=2\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ -12\\beta &=1\\, && (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\alpha =-\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\,\\beta =-\\frac{1}{12}.\n$$\n\nHence, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{1}{12}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357657 :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is  $2x-3.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is of the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are the constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation and group the terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}\\cdot 0+2x(\\alpha )-6(\\alpha x+\\beta ) & =2x-3 \\\\ (2\\alpha -6\\alpha )x-6\\beta  & =2x-3 \\\\ -4\\alpha x-6\\beta  & =2x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha &=2\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ -6\\beta &=-3\\, && (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\,\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nHence, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is  $2x-3.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is of the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are the constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation and group the terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}\\cdot 0+2x(\\alpha )-6(\\alpha x+\\beta ) & =2x-3 \\\\ (2\\alpha -6\\alpha )x-6\\beta  & =2x-3 \\\\ -4\\alpha x-6\\beta  & =2x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha &=2\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ -6\\beta &=-3\\, && (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\,\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357658 :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is  $5x-2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is of the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are the constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation and group the terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}\\cdot 0+4x(\\alpha )-16(\\alpha x+\\beta ) & =5x-2 \\\\ (4\\alpha -16\\alpha )x-16\\beta  & =5x-2 \\\\ -12\\alpha x-16\\beta  & =5x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-12\\alpha &=5\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ -16\\beta &=-2\\, && (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{5}{12},\\,\\,\\beta =\\frac{1}{8}.$\n\nHence, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{5x}{12}+\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$" "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is  $5x-2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is of the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are the constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation and group the terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}\\cdot 0+4x(\\alpha )-16(\\alpha x+\\beta ) & =5x-2 \\\\ (4\\alpha -16\\alpha )x-16\\beta  & =5x-2 \\\\ -12\\alpha x-16\\beta  & =5x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-12\\alpha &=5\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ -16\\beta &=-2\\, && (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\alpha =-\\frac{5}{12},\\,\\,\\beta =\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nHence, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{5x}{12}+\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357689 :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+6x{y}^{′}+6y=-{e}^{x},\\,x>0\n$$\n\nhas the complimentary function ${y}_{c}(x)=\\frac{A}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{2}}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation. \n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results:* \n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{x}\\text{d}x=({x}^{2}-2x+2){e}^{x}+C\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\int x{e}^{x}\\text{d}x=(x-1){e}^{x}+C\n$$" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+6x{y}^{′}+6y=-{e}^{x},\\,x>0\n$$\n\nhas the complimentary function $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{c}(x)=\\frac{A}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{2}},\n$$ find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation. \n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results:* \n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{x}\\text{d}x=({x}^{2}-2x+2){e}^{x}+C\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\int x{e}^{x}\\text{d}x=(x-1){e}^{x}+C\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357748 :question/problem "Given the elliptic cone $\\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{8}=\\frac{(z+6)^{2}}{6},$ find its intercepts with the $y$-axis." "Given the elliptic cone $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{8}=\\frac{(z+6)^{2}}{6},\n$$ find its intercepts with the $y$-axis."] [:db/cas 17592186357749 :question/problem "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the elliptic cone $\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}={z}^{2}\\,?$" "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the elliptic cone $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}={z}^{2}\\,?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186360457 :question/problem "Given the elliptic cone $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2},$ find its intercepts with the $y$-axis." "Given the elliptic cone $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2},\n$$ find its intercepts with the $y$-axis."] [:db/cas 17592186360458 :question/problem "Given the elliptic cone $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}={z}^{2},$ find its intercept with the $z$-axis." "Given the elliptic cone $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}={z}^{2},\n$$ find its intercept with the $z$-axis."] [:db/cas 17592186360463 :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $F={A\\in {\\text{M}}_{2}(R)\\,|\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}A=A\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $$\n\\displaystyle F={A\\in {\\text{M}}_{2}(R)\\,|\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}A=A\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186360463 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is closed under matrix addition” is true. If ${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}$ are two matrices from $F,$ then ${A}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}{A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}{A}_{2}.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}_{1}+{A}_{2})\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & ={A}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+{A}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}{A}_{1}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}({A}_{1}+{A}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${A}_{1}+{A}_{2}$ is a matrix from $F.$ So, $F$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is true. If $A$ is an element of $F,$ then $A\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}A.$ For $k\\in R,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(kA)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & =k\\cdot A\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =k\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}(kA).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $kA$ is a matrix from $F.$ So, $F$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” is true, while statement “$F$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” is false. $F$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$F$ is closed under matrix addition”; “$F$ is closed under scalar multiplication”; “$F$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” only." "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is closed under matrix addition” is true. If ${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}$ are two matrices from $F,$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {A}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}{A}_{1}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {A}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}{A}_{2}.\n$$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}_{1}+{A}_{2})\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & ={A}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+{A}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}{A}_{1}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}({A}_{1}+{A}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${A}_{1}+{A}_{2}$ is a matrix from $F.$ So, $F$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is true. If $A$ is an element of $F,$ then $A\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}A.$ For $k\\in R,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(kA)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & =k\\cdot A\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =k\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}(kA).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $kA$ is a matrix from $F.$ So, $F$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” is true, while statement “$F$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” is false. $F$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$F$ is closed under matrix addition”; “$F$ is closed under scalar multiplication”; “$F$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186360470 :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{10}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis." "Calculate the points where the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{10}=1\n$$ intercepts the $y$-axis."] [:db/cas 17592186360492 :question/worked-solution "If the set is linearly dependent, then there exist ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ not all zero such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}A+{x}_{2}B+{x}_{3}C=O.\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ the set is linearly independent.\n\nSubstituting the matrices $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{1} \\\\ -{x}_{1} & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} & -{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} & 3{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}7{x}_{3} & {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} & 6{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3} & {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} \\\\ -{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} & 3{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding augmented matrix and reduce it to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns are  the $1$st and $2$nd. Hence, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. \n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}-6{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the $2$nd equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-2{x}_{3}.$ Substitute it into the the first equation to get \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-4{x}_{3}+7{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-3{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3{x}_{3} \\\\ -2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.$\n\nFinally, for any ${x}_{3}\\ne 0,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}=\\frac{-3{x}_{3}}{-2{x}_{3}}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" "If the set is linearly dependent, then there exist ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ not all zero such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}A+{x}_{2}B+{x}_{3}C=O.\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ the set is linearly independent.\n\nSubstituting the matrices $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{1} \\\\ -{x}_{1} & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} & -{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} & 3{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}7{x}_{3} & {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} & 6{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3} & {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} \\\\ -{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} & 3{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding augmented matrix and reduce it to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns are  the $1$st and $2$nd. Hence, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. \n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}-6{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the $2$nd equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-2{x}_{3}.$ Substitute it into the the first equation to get \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-4{x}_{3}+7{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-3{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3{x}_{3} \\\\ -2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, for any ${x}_{3}\\ne 0,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}=\\frac{-3{x}_{3}}{-2{x}_{3}}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186360522 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement “$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}$” is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=(-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” is false. For example, the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{22}=0:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+b\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+c\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a & 6b \\\\ -2c & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$”; “$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}$” only." "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement “$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$” is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=(-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” is false. For example, the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{22}=0:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+b\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+c\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a & 6b \\\\ -2c & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$”; “$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186360523 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{4}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}={x}_{4}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement “$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$” is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+(-5)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+8\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” is true. Indeed, the set is linearly independent and for any matrix in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}=a\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+b\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+c\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+d\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$”; “$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$”; “$\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$”." "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{4}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}={x}_{4}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement “$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$” is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+(-5)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+8\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” is true. Indeed, the set is linearly independent and for any matrix in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}=a\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+b\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+c\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+d\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$”; “$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$”; “$$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$”."] [:db/cas 17592186360534 :question/problem "Which of the following best describes the projection of the intersection between the elliptic cone $\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=(z-2)^{2}$ and the plane $2z-y-6=0$ onto the $xy$-plane?" "Which of the following best describes the projection of the intersection between the elliptic cone $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=(z-2)^{2}\n$$ and the plane $2z-y-6=0$ onto the $xy$-plane?"] [:db/cas 17592186360539 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} & -3{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} & 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 9{x}_{2} &=3 \\\\ -6{x}_{2} &=-2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =1-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{2}{3}.$" "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} & -3{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} & 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 9{x}_{2} &=3 \\\\ -6{x}_{2} &=-2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =1-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186360567 :question/problem "Given that $w(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2},$ find $\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle w(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2},\n$$ find $\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates."] [:db/cas 17592186360624 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{e}^{x}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2x}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{e}^{x}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2x}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186360625 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{e}^{x}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2x}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{e}^{x}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2x}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186360626 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{7{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{7{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186360627 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{4x}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\di